<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estella-Lizarra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Estella</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Estella-Lizarra is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the Ega river and the pilgrim route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a> in 1090. Rich in Romanesque churches, it was a stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlism">Carlism</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war">Third Carlist War</a> it served as the capital of the Carlist state until its fall in February 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estella-lizarra.jpg" alt="Estella-Lizarra" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pompilos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayuntamiento_de_Estella_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Estella, in Basque Lizarra, lies south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> in the autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a> and Álava. It stands near a great meander of the Ega. The town was founded in 1090, when the place beside the fortified settlement of Lizarra received a charter from the Pamplonese king Sancho Ramirez.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town became a landmark on the Way of St. James, the pilgrimage route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, and thrived on its position and on the mix of peoples settled there. Navarrese kings called in settlers, chiefly Occitans from Auvergne and Limousin, and they lived alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and the original Navarrese inhabitants. The wealth that resulted produced a flourishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>, represented by the Church of San Pedro de la Rúa, the Palacio de los Reyes de Navarra and the Church of San Miguel, among others.</p><p>A Jewish community is recorded from the 11th century. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> came because of the privileges granted there, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a>. The Jews were expelled from Estella in 1498. In the 19th century the town was long the headquarters of Don Carlos, who was proclaimed king there in 1833, and a major base of the Carlists in their wars.</p><p>Tomás de Zumalacárregui was appointed commander in chief there. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war">Third Carlist War</a> began in 1872 Estella became the court of Carlos de Borbón y Austria-Este, called Carlos VII by his supporters, and virtually the capital of Carlist Spain until liberal troops took the town on 16 February 1876, when the Carlists there surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Between 1927 and 1967 Estella was the terminus of the Ferrocarril Vasco-Navarro, a railway running as far as Bergara, which was electrified in 1938, a feat for the time. The town holds the cycling race GP Miguel Induráin each year. Its Romanesque churches and palace are the chief remains of its medieval prosperity on the pilgrim road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estella-Lizarra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These francos came with the pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, which crossed the kingdom from Roncesvalles through Pamplona, Puente la Reina and <b>Estella</b>; kings granted them charters and privileges, and towns such as Estella, founded around 1090, grew on the trade of the pilgrim road.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carlists organised a real administration from <b>Estella</b>: ministries, courts, a postal service with its own stamps, coinage and a university at Oñati.</blockquote><a href="/state/carlist-state">Carlist State</a></li></ul></section>
